# @corpus current
# THE ANCHOR — the half of the `@` token that adjacency did NOT
# reconcile, and that still has no meaning.
#
# GAP-059 row 2 was closed by giving the GLUED spelling to the anchor:
# `p@x` is a suffix now, it retrieves, and `examples/anchor/move.id`
# pins it by value at 353. `infix_prec` still maps the `@` token to `.matmul`,
# so the SPACED spelling kept everything the glued one shed:
#
#     print(p @ x)   ->   warning: infix '@' matmul is non-canonical
#                    ->   ✓ checked — no errors        exit 0
#                    ->   error: use of undeclared identifier 'x'
#
# A green `duo check` on a construct the compiler has no meaning for is the
# defect, and adjacency narrowed it rather than removing it: `x` here is the
# right OPERAND of a tensor product over a record and an undeclared name.
# Nothing downstream has a meaning for that, and saying so at check time is
# the whole of what §2 licenses — the anchor already has its spelling one
# space to the left.
#
# This is a REJECTION fixture, not a value fixture, and that is not a
# placeholder. `anchormove.id` is the value fixture for what `@` DOES mean;
# there is no second meaning left for this file to assert. Its subject is a
# refusal, so the pin has to be the diagnostic.
#
# The tensor product stays legal and is why the verdict lives in sema rather
# than the parser: `Tensor[M,K] @ Tensor[K,N]` is shape-checked, has three
# fixtures beside this one (tensor_matmul_k_mismatch.id and friends), and is
# the only spelling that operation has. The parser sees one token and no
# types; sema sees the operands.

point: { x: i64, y: i64 }

main(): i64
    p: point = { x = 3, y = 4 }
    print(p @ x)
    0
end
